question:
Let \((a_n)\) and \((b_n)\) be sequences of non‑negative real numbers with \(a_n \le b_n\) for every \(n\in\mathbb{N}\). Using the fact that a series of non‑negative terms converges if and only if its sequence of partial sums is bounded, prove that:

(i) if \(\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} b_n\) converges then \(\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n\) converges;

(ii) if \(\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} a_n\) diverges then \(\displaystyle\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} b_n\) diverges.

In the comparison test for series with non‑negative terms, suppose \((x_n)\) and \((y_n)\) satisfy \(y_n \ge x_n \ge 0\) for all \(n\). Explain why the two statements\n    \[
      \text{(i) If }\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} y_n \text{ converges, then }\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} x_n \text{ converges,}
    \]\n    \[
      \text{(ii) If }\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} x_n \text{ diverges, then }\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} y_n \text{ diverges,}
    \]\n    are logically equivalent. In your explanation, explicitly use the contrapositive and the fact that the terms are non‑negative.

answer:
The choice \(\delta=\min\!\left\{\frac{c}{2},\,\frac{c^{2}}{2\varepsilon}\right\}\) is designed to control two aspects of the expression \(\bigl|\frac{1}{x}-\frac{1}{c}\bigr|\). First, requiring \(|x-c|<\frac{c}{2}\) ensures that \(x\) stays away from zero: since \(c\in(0,1)\), we have \(x>c-\frac{c}{2}=\frac{c}{2}>0\). This gives a lower bound on the denominator \(x\) and allows us to estimate\n    \[
    \bigl|\tfrac{1}{x}-\tfrac{1}{c}\bigr|
      =\frac{|x-c|}{|x\,c|}
      \le \frac{|x-c|}{\frac{c}{2}\,c}
      =\frac{2|x-c|}{c^{2}}.
    \]\n    Second, the condition \(|x-c|<\frac{c^{2}}{2\varepsilon}\) ensures that the right‑hand side of the above inequality is less than \(\varepsilon\): substituting the bound on \(|x-c|\) gives\n    \[
    \frac{2|x-c|}{c^{2}}
      < \frac{2}{c^{2}}\cdot\frac{c^{2}}{2\varepsilon}
      =\varepsilon.
    \]\n    By taking \(\delta\) to be the minimum of these two numbers, we simultaneously guarantee that \(x\) stays in the interval \((0,1)\) (so the function is defined) and that the difference \(\bigl|\frac{1}{x}-\frac{1}{c}\bigr|\) is smaller than the prescribed \(\varepsilon\). Thus the chosen \(\delta\) satisfies the \(\varepsilon\)–\(\delta\) condition for continuity at the point \(c\).

If \(f\) and \(h\) are differentiable at a point \(x\), then the limits defining \(f'(x)\) and \(h'(x)\) exist.  The sum \(f+h\) has derivative\n\[
(f+h)'(x)=\lim_{h\to0}\frac{f(x+h)+h(x+h)-f(x)-h(x)}{h}
          =\lim_{h\to0}\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}
           +\lim_{h\to0}\frac{h(x+h)-h(x)}{h}
          =f'(x)+h'(x).
\]\nBecause both \(f'(x)\) and \(h'(x)\) exist, their sum exists, so \((f+h)'(x)\) exists.  Thus \(f+h\) is differentiable at every point where \(f\) and \(h\) are differentiable, i.e., everywhere if both functions are differentiable everywhere.  This reasoning uses the linearity of limits and the fact that differentiability implies continuity.
